WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.750
Merge sort builds an ordered row from smaller ordered pieces. Follow these same four values through every split, copy, and return.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:20.542
Split the index range into left and right children. The values have not moved. We will show both children together so you can compare their work.

00:00:20.542 --> 00:00:33.667
Each child splits into single values. Each single value is already sorted. This is a side by side overview. The Java code actually finishes the left child before the right.

00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:47.875
On the left, is seven greater than three? True, so copy three. On the right, is five greater than two? True, so copy two. Both copies go into their own temporary output.

00:00:47.875 --> 00:00:57.458
Both right inputs are empty. Copy the leftover seven on the left, and five on the right. Neither copy needs another comparison.

00:00:57.458 --> 00:01:10.458
Copy each completed result back into its own array range. The left child returns three, seven. The right child returns two, five. Both are now ready for the parent.

00:01:10.458 --> 00:01:19.500
Is 3 greater than 2? True. Copy 2 from the right into temporary output. Only that pointer advances.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:28.667
Is 3 greater than 5? False. Copy 3 from the left into temporary output. Only that pointer advances.

00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:37.583
Is 7 greater than 5? True. Copy 5 from the right into temporary output. Only that pointer advances.

00:01:37.583 --> 00:01:44.708
One side is empty. Copy the remaining 7. This is not another comparison.

00:01:44.708 --> 00:01:51.542
Copy the completed output back into the original range. Only now is the whole array sorted.

00:01:51.542 --> 00:02:06.000
Five comparisons, eight temporary copies, and eight copy back writes. Merging is not swapping. On equal values, greater than is false, so choose the left first to preserve their order.
